## Groundcover connects Claude agent traces to application telemetry

Source: groundcover — https://www.groundcover.com/blog/claude-platform-tracing-groundcover
Published: 2026-11-22
Channel: news

Groundcover is an observability platform that collects traces and infrastructure telemetry. Its new integration ingests Claude Platform traces so engineers can inspect model requests and agent tool use alongside the services those agents call. Groundcover says that previously the agent loop and the application infrastructure could live in separate views, making it harder to follow a request across them.

An administrator first adds a groundcover endpoint as a tracing destination in the Claude Console and selects the workspaces and content mode. The application exporter sends its own telemetry to the same endpoint. When the application passes trace context, the Claude platform’s spans—records of individual operations—nest under the application’s spans. Groundcover says its normalizer maps the incoming data into its existing AI views, where teams can inspect turns, model requests and tool calls together.

Full content includes prompts and responses; metadata-only mode retains details such as timing and token counts without that content. Groundcover says its pipeline can mask or remove fields before storage, and its customer-cloud deployment stores the data in the customer’s infrastructure. The platform tracing integration is a public beta; the company says Claude requests through Amazon Bedrock do not yet export platform spans.

Why it matters: Teams operating Claude agents can check trace-context propagation and choose content capture and masking settings before using the combined view for debugging.

